      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2091.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 550,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on Earth.** It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical Composition\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   Two **hydrogen** atoms\n*   One **oxygen** atom\nThese atoms are bonded together by covalent bonds, creating a molecule with a bent shape. Because oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, water is a **polar molecule**—meaning the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity is what makes water such a great solvent (things dissolve in it easily).\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and find in rivers and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Uniquely, solid water (ice) is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the air.\n\n### 3. Role on Earth\n*   **The \"Blue Planet\":** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrological cycle. It evaporates from the surface, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into oceans and rivers.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is the medium in which the metabolic and cellular processes of life take place. \n*   **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and transports nutrients through the blood.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of aquatic species and is required by plants to perform photosynthesis (how they make food).\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the driving force of nature, weather, and life itself."
